-- gap[104] · A BRANCH THE COMPILER PROVES CONSTANT MUST STILL RUN ITS BODY.
--
-- WHAT THIS GATE IS FOR. `if 42 > 10 print("BIG")` compiled clean, exited 0 and
-- printed nothing. `if/else` on a constant could execute NEITHER arm. The cause
-- was never the constant and never the branch: a block's final expression lives
-- in `blk.tail_expr`, not in `blk.stmts`, and on the direct backend nothing
-- lowered it unless the block was the function's answer. The branch was taken
-- the whole time — `if 42 > 10 return 7` always exited 7.
--
-- WHY IT IS A SEPARATE GATE FROM `tail-slot`. That gate proves the MECHANISM is
-- repaired, on four fixtures aimed at the lowering. This one proves the
-- SYMPTOM cannot come back, across the ten shapes a constant condition actually
-- takes in source — including the one nobody can grep for. A future change that
-- reintroduces the hole by another route (a new fold, a new block lowerer, an
-- inliner) fails here even if it leaves `lowerBlockTailEffect` untouched.
--
-- THE BLAST RADIUS, which is the reason for the fixture list. The defect is not
-- "programs containing a literal condition". It is "programs the optimizer
-- proves a condition constant in", and constant propagation creates that shape
-- from source that contained no literal at all. `folded.duo` is that case: its
-- body is written against a parameter and the constant arrives from the call
-- site. It is the row this gate exists for; the literal rows are the ones that
-- made the defect findable.
--
-- EVERY ROW IS ASSERTED BY VALUE. "It compiled" and "it did not crash" are not
-- evidence here — this defect's whole signature is a clean compile, a zero exit
-- and a silently wrong answer. Rows compare captured STDOUT byte for byte and
-- read the exit code separately, never inferring one from the other.
--
-- THE ROWS
--
--   P0  the fixtures exist and the count is what the rows assume   control
--   H0  the comparator can tell two different outputs apart        harness
--   B1  literal true                                               gap[104]
--   B2  literal false — the CLASSIFIER control                     negative
--   B3  true with else                                             gap[104]
--   B4  false with else                                            gap[104]
--   B5  a compile-time-known local                                 gap[104]
--   B6  a DERIVED constant — two levels of fold                    gap[104]
--   B7  a constant condition nested in a constant condition        gap[104]
--   B8  the branch as the ANSWER, read by EXIT CODE                negative
--   B9  a branch that mutates state — wrong NUMBER, not just a
--       missing line                                               gap[104]
--   B10 an OPTIMIZER-CREATED constant condition                    gap[104]
--   X1  direct and the C backend agree on every fixture            oracle
--
-- MEASURED AGAINST THE UNFIXED LOWERING, which is what makes these a regression
-- test rather than ten programs that happen to pass. With the tail-effect
-- lowering defeated, EIGHT of the ten rows produce a different, wrong answer:
--
--   truelit     first|after|                  `last` gone
--   truearms    then|after|                   the arm's tail gone
--   falsearms   else|after|                   the other arm's tail gone
--   constlocal  big|after|
--   derived     derived|after|
--   nested      inner|after|                  BOTH tail slots gone
--   effects     3|7|                          the branch's second print gone
--   folded      folded|after|after|
--
-- The two that do NOT move are the two negative controls, and that is by
-- design: `falselit` has no taken body to lose, and `tailbranch` reports through
-- the exit code, which the defect never touched. A gate whose every row moves
-- when a fix is defeated is not measuring its own controls.

rows = ""
failed = 0

here = (): str
    std.script.chomp(std.script.capture("pwd"))

quiet = (cmd: str): i64
    std.script.status("{cmd} >/dev/null 2>&1")

grab = (cmd: str): str
    std.script.chomp(std.script.capture(cmd))

note = (tag: str, ok: bool, detail: str): void
    mark = "FAIL"
    if ok
        mark = "ok  "
    if not ok
        failed = failed + 1
    rows = "{rows}  {mark} {tag}  {detail}\n"

-- One fixture, one backend: compile, run, and return the exit code and the
-- whole of stdout as a single comparable value with newlines rendered `|`.
--
-- The artifact and its output file are REMOVED first. A stale capture left by
-- the previous backend reads exactly like a pass, and a gate that reports green
-- off the other backend's bytes is the confident-green this file exists to
-- refuse. `duo compile` writes beside the source unless `-o` says otherwise, so
-- every product is named into the work directory — a build artifact in the repo
-- root is a hygiene finding.
run = (bin: str, work: str, name: str, backend: str): str
    src = "examples/constbranch/{name}.duo"
    exe = "{work}/{name}.{backend}"
    out = "{work}/{name}.{backend}.out"
    quiet("rm -f {exe} {out}")
    built = quiet("{bin} compile {src} --backend={backend} -o {exe}")
    if built != 0
        return "BUILD-FAILED"
    code = std.script.status("{exe} >{out} 2>/dev/null")
    body = grab("tr '\\n' '|' <{out}")
    "rc={code} {body}"

main = (): i64
    root = here()
    bin = "{root}/zig-out/bin/duo"
    work = "/tmp/duo-constbranch"
    quiet("rm -rf {work}")
    quiet("mkdir -p {work}")

    -- P0. A gate that scores 0/N over 0 subjects is broken, not green. Count the
    -- fixtures on disk before trusting a single row below.
    fixtures = grab("ls examples/constbranch/*.duo | wc -l | tr -d ' '")
    have = to(i64)(fixtures)
    note("P0 control", have == 10, "fixtures on disk: {have} of 10")
    if have != 10
        print(rows)
        print("constbranch: FAIL — the fixture set is not what the rows below assume")
        return 3

    truelitd = run(bin, work, "truelit", "direct")
    truelitc = run(bin, work, "truelit", "c")
    falselitd = run(bin, work, "falselit", "direct")
    falselitc = run(bin, work, "falselit", "c")
    truearmsd = run(bin, work, "truearms", "direct")
    truearmsc = run(bin, work, "truearms", "c")
    falsearmsd = run(bin, work, "falsearms", "direct")
    falsearmsc = run(bin, work, "falsearms", "c")
    localsd = run(bin, work, "constlocal", "direct")
    localsc = run(bin, work, "constlocal", "c")
    derivedd = run(bin, work, "derived", "direct")
    derivedc = run(bin, work, "derived", "c")
    nestedd = run(bin, work, "nested", "direct")
    nestedc = run(bin, work, "nested", "c")
    tailbd = run(bin, work, "tailbranch", "direct")
    tailbc = run(bin, work, "tailbranch", "c")
    effectsd = run(bin, work, "effects", "direct")
    effectsc = run(bin, work, "effects", "c")
    foldedd = run(bin, work, "folded", "direct")
    foldedc = run(bin, work, "folded", "c")

    -- H0. POSITIVE-CONTROL THE COMPARATOR ITSELF. Every row below is an equality
    -- against a literal, so a comparator that answered true for everything would
    -- report ten greens over ten broken programs. Two fixtures that are known to
    -- differ must compare unequal, or nothing else printed here means anything.
    note("H0 harness", truelitd != falselitd,
        "two different outputs compare unequal: `{truelitd}` vs `{falselitd}`")

    -- B1. The filed repro. `first|after|` is the defect — the body's tail
    -- expression lost — and `after|` alone would mean the whole body went.
    note("B1 literal true", truelitd == "rc=0 first|last|after|",
        "if 42 > 10 · direct: {truelitd}")

    -- B2. THE CLASSIFIER CONTROL, and the reason B1 means anything. If constant
    -- conditions were simply never evaluated, this body would run and print
    -- `never`. A green here plus a green on B1 says the fold DECIDES and then
    -- emits the arm it decided on.
    note("B2 literal false", falselitd == "rc=0 after|",
        "if 42 < 10 skips, and only skips · direct: {falselitd}")

    -- B3/B4. Both arms, on opposite verdicts, with the `if/else` pushed out of
    -- the function's tail slot by a trailing print. Under the defect BOTH arms
    -- were lost — the observation no mis-taken-branch theory could explain.
    note("B3 true with else", truearmsd == "rc=0 then|thenlast|after|",
        "the taken arm runs whole · direct: {truearmsd}")
    note("B4 false with else", falsearmsd == "rc=0 else|elselast|after|",
        "the else arm runs whole · direct: {falsearmsd}")

    -- B5/B6. The shape the gap was filed as, and the shape that proves the fold
    -- reaches more than one level. `derived` needs `a + b` folded before
    -- `c > 10` is decidable at all.
    note("B5 constant local", localsd == "rc=0 big|biglast|after|",
        "n = 42 · if n > 10 · direct: {localsd}")
    note("B6 derived constant", derivedd == "rc=0 derived|derivedlast|after|",
        "c = a + b · if c > 10 · direct: {derivedd}")

    -- B7. Two tail slots stacked. A repair that handles one level and not the
    -- recursive case loses `outerlast` alone, which is one line of difference —
    -- so the row prints four distinguishable strings rather than one.
    note("B7 nested condition", nestedd == "rc=0 inner|innerlast|outerlast|after|",
        "a constant condition inside one · direct: {nestedd}")

    -- B8. THE NEGATIVE CONTROL THAT SETTLED THE DIAGNOSIS. Reported by exit
    -- code, not by output: three readings of this defect were withdrawn because
    -- each probed control flow with `print` while `print` was the broken
    -- instrument. rc=7 says the true arm ran; rc=3 says it did not. It is also
    -- the check that an `if` which IS the answer may still return.
    note("B8 tail branch", tailbd == "rc=7 ",
        "if 42 > 10 return 7 · answers by EXIT CODE · direct: {tailbd}")

    -- B9. State, so a lost body is a wrong NUMBER and not only a missing line.
    -- A body run twice reads 3|7|10|14|14, a body skipped reads 0, a body whose
    -- tail is dropped reads 3|7 — three distinct wrong answers, none of them the
    -- expected one.
    note("B9 side effects", effectsd == "rc=0 3|7|7|",
        "the branch mutates and emits in order · direct: {effectsd}")

    -- B10. THE ROW THE GATE IS FOR. Nothing in `gate`'s body is a literal
    -- condition; the constant arrives at `k > 10` from the call site. Both
    -- verdicts come out of the same body, so this one fixture carries the true
    -- case, the false case, and the proof they are decided per call.
    note("B10 folded constant", foldedd == "rc=0 folded|foldedlast|after|after|",
        "a constant condition the SOURCE DOES NOT CONTAIN · direct: {foldedd}")

    -- X1. The oracle. §3 MEASUREMENT HONESTY: the two lowerings must produce the
    -- SAME ANSWER, and that is checked before any row above is worth anything.
    -- The C backend has no tail-slot split and was correct throughout, which is
    -- exactly why `--backend=c` masked this defect for as long as it did.
    agree = truelitd == truelitc and falselitd == falselitc and
        truearmsd == truearmsc and falsearmsd == falsearmsc and
        localsd == localsc and derivedd == derivedc and
        nestedd == nestedc and tailbd == tailbc and
        effectsd == effectsc and foldedd == foldedc
    note("X1 oracle", agree, "direct vs --backend=c on all ten fixtures")

    print(rows)
    if failed > 0
        print("constbranch: FAIL — {failed} row(s); a proven-constant branch is losing its body")
        return 1
    print("constbranch: ok — a branch the compiler proves constant runs its body, on both backends")
    0
